def b2c_assets() -> list[dict[str, str]]:
    common = {
        "audience_state": "A person feels behind, distrusts grand productivity promises, and can still finish one small visible thing.",
        "media_direction": "Use one real desk or phone, a handwritten start line, a twenty-minute clock, and a paired before-and-after receipt; keep natural sound and visible imperfections.",
        "earned_distribution_mechanism": "The participant receives a private finished artifact first and may share a redacted receipt that names the tiny task, not a performance score or aspirational identity.",
        "proof_unit": "A timestamped private before-and-after pair, the user's own finish sentence, and optional redacted Twenty-Minute Receipt.",
        "metric": "Timer starts, private completions, next-day repeat use, voluntary saves or shares, paid conversion, and regret or pressure signals.",
        "kill_rule": "Stop the lane if it creates shame, compulsive streak behavior, unsafe goals, fabricated transformation, high shares without repeat use, or weak completion quality.",
    }
    rows = [
        ("linkedin", "Reach skeptical knowledge workers through an anti-transformation point of view.", "reflective point-of-view post with a real receipt", "I stopped asking AI to fix my life. I gave it twenty minutes.", "I stopped asking AI to fix my life. I gave it twenty minutes.\n\nThe task was not ambitious: turn a page of scattered meeting notes into three decisions I could send. Before starting, I wrote one finish line: “Three owners can act without asking what I meant.” Then I ran a twenty-minute clock.\n\nAt minute eighteen, the artifact was not beautiful, but it was sendable. The useful output was the pair: the messy page, the three decisions, and one sentence about what changed. No streak. No productivity score. No claim that tomorrow will be different.\n\nWe call that pair a Twenty-Minute Receipt. It is private by default. If you share it, redact names and show the work—not a performance persona. For people exhausted by infinite optimization, a small honest finish can be a better product moment than another plan.", "Name one safe, finishable result and borrow twenty minutes; keep the receipt private unless sharing helps you."),
        ("tiktok", "Make a modest, credible transformation visible in one watch.", "timed one-take before-and-after demonstration", "Not a new life. One finished thing before the kettle cools.", "[00:00] Camera on a chaotic note. Text: NOT FIXING MY LIFE.\n[00:03] Hand writes: finish line = three decisions with owners.\n[00:06] Twenty-minute clock starts; phone flips face down.\n[00:10] Jump cuts show deleting, grouping, naming—not motivational montage.\n[00:17] Timer at 02:11; one decision is still vague. Rewrite it.\n[00:23] Show the sendable page beside the original.\n[00:27] Receipt reads: borrowed 20 minutes, finished one thing. No streak added.", "Try one private twenty-minute finish and share only a redacted before-and-after if you choose."),
        ("instagram", "Create a saveable ritual without productivity theater.", "six-card carousel and optional reel receipt", "Borrow twenty minutes. Return one finished thing.", "Card 1: BORROW 20 MINUTES. Card 2: Pick a finish line small enough to photograph. Card 3: Write what “done” looks like before the clock starts. Card 4: Hide every task that is not this task. Card 5: Stop at twenty, even if the result is imperfect. Card 6: Place before beside after and write one honest sentence. Caption: the receipt is evidence of a finish, not evidence that you are a better person. Keep it private, save the ritual, or share a redacted pair with someone who needs permission to start smaller.", "Save the six-card ritual and make one private receipt today."),
        ("reddit", "Invite honest critique from people harmed by productivity systems.", "discussion postmortem with counterexample request", "Postmortem: a twenty-minute focus test worked only after we removed the streak.", "Title: Postmortem: our twenty-minute focus test worked only after we removed the streak.\n\nThe first prototype congratulated users, counted consecutive days, and suggested the next task. Completion rose for three days; pressure complaints rose faster. We rebuilt it around a private before-and-after receipt: name one finishable result, stop after twenty minutes, write one sentence, and leave. No leaderboard, public score, or penalty for tomorrow.\n\nThe useful question is not “does a timer help?” It is where a small constraint becomes shame, avoidance, or another demand. I am sharing the boundary checklist and would value counterexamples from ADHD, disability, caregiving, and shift-work perspectives. No diagnosis or cure claims.", "Read the boundary checklist and add one context where the ritual should adapt or stop."),
        ("x", "Deliver a compact anti-hustle protocol with an honest proof unit.", "numbered seven-part thread", "1/ You do not need a better life plan for the next twenty minutes.", "1/ You do not need a better life plan for the next twenty minutes.\n2/ Pick one result small enough to see: send three decisions, clear one surface, outline one reply.\n3/ Write the finish line before the timer starts.\n4/ Hide every task that is not this one.\n5/ Stop at 20. Imperfect is allowed.\n6/ Put before beside after. Add one honest sentence. That is the receipt.\n7/ Keep it private. Repeat tomorrow only if it still helps. No streak owns you.", "Borrow twenty minutes for one visible finish; save the receipt for yourself first."),
        ("facebook", "Offer a gentle household or community ritual people can use together.", "community story with printable private receipt", "Tonight we tried a family rule: one tiny finish, twenty minutes, nobody grades it.", "One person sorted the school forms. One cleared a chair that had become a second closet. One drafted the message they had avoided. Before starting, each wrote what done would look like; after twenty minutes, everyone stopped. We did not compare tasks or build a streak. The surprise was not speed. It was that a small boundary made asking for help easier: “Can you hold this while I finish the one thing?” The printable receipt has a private side and an optional share side with redaction prompts. Use it for ordinary, safe tasks—not health crises, dangerous work, or anything that needs professional care.", "Print one private receipt and choose a tiny household finish nobody will score."),
        ("pinterest", "Create durable search utility for people seeking a small focus ritual.", "searchable printable routine pin", "A twenty-minute reset printable with no streak and no shame.", "Pin title: Twenty-Minute Receipt — a no-streak focus printable. Visual: start box, one finish line, simple timer, before-and-after frames, one reflection sentence, and a prominent KEEP PRIVATE option. Description: choose one safe task small enough to complete or meaningfully advance in twenty minutes. Define done first, stop on time, and compare the artifact rather than your worth. Includes accessible large-print and low-ink versions.", "Save the printable and use the private version for one safe task."),
        ("bluesky", "Invite thoughtful alternatives to coercive productivity patterns.", "compact open-method post and reply thread", "A focus tool should be allowed to end without asking for a streak.", "A focus tool should be allowed to end without asking for a streak. Our test is deliberately small: name one visible finish, borrow twenty minutes, stop, and keep a private before-and-after receipt. No score, leaderboard, motivational persona, or claim that the ritual works for everyone. We are publishing the boundary notes and accessible variants. Interested in what this misses for people with fluctuating energy, care work, or tasks where progress is not visible.", "Review the boundary notes and suggest one humane adaptation, not a growth hack."),
        ("mastodon", "Share an accessible, non-coercive protocol with federated communities.", "federated thread with alt text and content warning for productivity themes", "A tiny focus ritual, designed so opting out still counts as a valid decision.", "CW: productivity pressure. The Twenty-Minute Receipt asks for one safe, visible finish and stops after twenty minutes. No streak, score, leaderboard, or public default. Images include alt text describing the handwritten finish line, clock state, and paired artifact; plain-text and large-print versions are linked. If the ritual increases distress or the task needs care, expertise, or more time, the correct result is to stop. Feedback from disability and neurodivergent communities is invited before any broader release.", "Use the plain-text private version or contribute one accessibility boundary."),
        ("seo", "Answer high-intent searches for a humane twenty-minute focus method.", "evidence-aware guide page with printable template", "How to use a twenty-minute focus session without streaks or productivity shame.", "Page H1: The Twenty-Minute Receipt. Direct answer: choose one safe result you can see, define done before starting, work for twenty minutes, stop, and compare the artifact rather than scoring yourself. This guide explains when the constraint helps, when to stop, how to split tasks, accessible variants, private-by-default handling, and why completion, repeat usefulness, and wellbeing matter more than a public streak.", "Download the private printable and choose one safe, visible finish."),
        ("geo", "Expose a concise, citable definition with limitations for answer engines.", "direct answer object with citation and limitation fields", "What is a Twenty-Minute Receipt?", "Direct answer: A Twenty-Minute Receipt is a private before-and-after record for one bounded focus session. The person defines a visible finish, works for up to twenty minutes, stops, and writes one sentence about what changed. It is not a diagnosis, treatment, productivity score, or promise of habit formation. A safe implementation includes opt-out, no-streak design, privacy defaults, accessible formats, and stop guidance for distressing or hazardous tasks.", "Use the private template or cite the definition together with its limitations."),
        ("email", "Support a user-requested experiment without creating notification pressure.", "requested single-message guide with optional reply", "Subject: Your private twenty-minute receipt — no streak attached", "You asked for the private receipt. Choose one safe result small enough to see, write what done means, and start the twenty-minute clock. Stop when it rings. Put before beside after and add one sentence. Nothing is posted, scored, or carried into a streak. If the session did not help, reply STOP REASON with the category—task too large, energy mismatch, interruption, distress, or other—and no follow-up sequence will be sent unless you request it.", "Run one private session, or reply STOP REASON so the sequence ends and the boundary can improve."),
        ("landing_page", "Deliver value before signup through a private proof experience.", "interactive hero with local-only conversion experience", "Don't fix your life. Borrow twenty minutes.", "Hero: Don't fix your life. Borrow twenty minutes. Subhead: choose one safe, visible finish and leave with a private before-and-after receipt—no streak, score, or public feed. Interactive proof: write the finish line locally, start the timer, attach optional before and after, and export a private receipt without an account. Below the fold: accessible modes, stop guidance, example receipts, privacy details, and an optional account only after the first session proves useful.", "Start a local-only twenty-minute session; decide about an account after the receipt exists."),
    ]
    return [{"surface_id": surface, "job": job, "native_format": fmt, "hook": hook, "publishable_copy": copy, "native_mechanic": fmt, "cta": cta, **common} for surface, job, fmt, hook, copy, cta in rows]


